      <title>Hackers behind $6.3bn Binance heist ‘used malware and bogus job interviews’</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/03/hackers-63bn-binance-heist-malware-bogus-job-interviews-cryptocurrency</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Firm reveals details about how its cryptocurrency exchange was compromised, saying operation began in June&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The hackers behind the &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/29/binance-crypto-exchange-hack"&gt;$6.3bn (£4.7bn) Binance heist&lt;/a&gt; used bogus job interviews, malicious software and an internal AI tool, the company has revealed.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The world’s largest cryptocurrency exchange was compromised in an operation that began with an inadvertent download of malware in June, led to a recruitment scam in September and then a raid last week of “hot wallets” where billions of dollars worth of crypto-assets were taken.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/03/hackers-63bn-binance-heist-malware-bogus-job-interviews-cryptocurrency"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
